# 📚 Laboratory Exercise

## 📌 Exercise 10 — Multi-Layer Perceptron for Time-Series Forecasting

**Notebook:** `Lab_10_22jzele0480.ipynb`

### Topics Covered

- Importing TensorFlow/Keras libraries
- Loading training, validation, and testing datasets
- Defining input time steps and feature dimensions
- Building an MLP architecture using:
  - Flatten Layer
  - Dense Hidden Layers
  - Output Layer
- Configuring optimizer and loss function
- Setting up ModelCheckpoint callbacks
- Training the MLP model
- Monitoring training and validation history
- Predicting future electricity demand
- Evaluating forecasting performance

**Learning Focus**

Develop a deep learning forecasting model capable of learning nonlinear relationships from historical electricity demand data and generating accurate future predictions.

---

# 🔍 Core Deep Learning Concepts

## 📌 Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP)

A Multi-Layer Perceptron is a feed-forward neural network composed of fully connected (**Dense**) layers.

The network receives historical time-series observations as input and learns nonlinear relationships to predict future values.

---

## 📌 Time-Series Forecasting

Time-series forecasting uses previous observations to estimate future values.

The forecasting model is trained using historical electricity demand data organized into fixed-length input windows.

---

## 📌 Model Checkpointing

Model checkpointing automatically saves the best-performing model during training based on validation performance.

This prevents overfitting and preserves the most accurate model.

---

## 📌 Forecast Evaluation

Model performance is evaluated using common regression metrics, including:

- Mean Absolute Error (MAE)
- Mean Squared Error (MSE)
- Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
- Coefficient of Determination (R² Score)
- Explained Variance Score

